title: GMO Onamae.com Desktop Cloud
page: breaches/gmo-onamae-desktop-cloud.html
as-of: 2026-11-21
promise: In GMO's account, an attacker got from a customer's remote desktop VPS to GMO's management server and pushed malware through it to customer desktops; saved broker logins were used for unauthorized trades, and extortion followed.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 26,739 | servers that may have been affected | not a count of confirmed unauthorized access, per GMO
stat: 2+ | months before detection | from the management server takeover, per GMO's press conference timeline
stat: $50M | US dollars, emailed demand, October 21 | in Bitcoin or Ether; about ¥8 billion per GMO, which refused to pay
GMO has not said how the attacker got in or took the administrator account, and names no attacker or country. Kyodo News reports a likely North Korea-linked group, citing unnamed sources and IP overlaps.
notes:
Start with the page's three figures, and with the caveats the page keeps beside them.

26,739 is GMO's count of servers that may have been affected. GMO says it is not the number of cases in which unauthorized access or information acquisition has been confirmed, so do not call it a count of victims.

More than two months passed between the takeover of GMO's management server on August 17 and detection, by the timeline GMO gave at its November 2 press conference.

The extortion email of October 21 demanded US$50 million in Bitcoin or Ether, about ¥8 billion by GMO's figure. GMO refused to pay.

The page sets three caveats next to the headline. The 26,739 figure is not a count of confirmed unauthorized access. GMO has not said how the attacker got in or obtained the administrator account. And GMO has named no attacker or country. Kyodo News reports that a North Korea-linked group was likely responsible, citing unnamed sources and IP address overlaps; a later slide sets that report beside GMO's position.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: August 5, 2026 | Entry through a customer's remote desktop VPS
event: August 17 | GMO's management server entered with an administrator account
event: September 20 to October 14 | Malware sent through that server to customer VMs, per @IT
event: October 19 and 20 | A customer inquiry, then GMO confirms a malicious program
event: October 21 | Every plan shut down; extortion email; first reports of unauthorized trades
event: October 27 | The attacker publishes what it claimed was GMO and customer data
Reports of GMO's timeline differ on whether the administrator account was taken on August 5 or August 17. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The timeline shows only dates the page gives. The markers are evenly spaced, and the gaps between them are not to scale.

GMO dated the first intrusion, in a customer's remote desktop VPS, to August 5, in the timeline it gave at its November 2 press conference as reported by @IT. Reports of that timeline differ on when the administrator account was taken: August 5 in ITmedia Business Online, Business+IT and MediaDogs, and August 17 in @IT's account. Every report has the management server entered on August 17.

Per @IT, malware went out through the management server to customer virtual machines, or VMs, from September 20 to October 14. A customer inquiry on October 19 started the investigation, and GMO confirmed the malicious program on October 20. From 04:00 JST on October 21, JST being Japan Standard Time, GMO shut down servers on every plan in sequence. The extortion email arrived that day, and customers began reporting trades they had not placed.

On October 27 GMO confirmed that the attacker had published what it claimed was information about GMO and its customers.

== What the attacker claimed, and what GMO has confirmed
layout: table
row: The attacker's claim, on its leak site | GMO's position, per the page
row: Entry through a Remote Desktop Protocol flaw | Not confirmed; GMO withheld the method
row: Active Directory across the VPS network | Not confirmed
row: An administrator password intercepted during a support engineer's remote connection | Not confirmed
row: The drive redirection claim | Confirmed by GMO at the press conference, per @IT; not in its notices
GovInfoSecurity reported the first three claims from the leak site. GMO has not said how the attacker got in or obtained the administrator account.
notes:
GMO has said little about how the attacker got in, and the attacker has said more. The page keeps the two apart.

GMO withheld the entry method. Hirofumi Yamashita, GMO Internet Group's executive vice president in charge of systems, cited the need to prevent similar attacks and the effect on the investigation. The attacker claimed on its leak site to have exploited a Remote Desktop Protocol flaw, according to GovInfoSecurity, and GMO has not confirmed that.

GMO has not said how the administrator account was obtained or what kind of account it was. The attacker's version, which GovInfoSecurity reported from the leak site, is that the VPS network ran as an Active Directory environment that let it move to other VPS servers, and that it intercepted an administrator password exposed when a support engineer connected remotely to a customer's PC. GMO has confirmed none of these claims.

The drive redirection claim also appeared on the leak site first. GMO later named the default setting as the reason files on customers' local PCs were taken, at the press conference as reported by @IT. Its written notices do not say so.

== Attribution: what Kyodo reports and what GMO says
layout: compare
col: Kyodo News reports | Unnamed sources: GMO found a North Korea-linked group likely responsible | Five IPs that reached the claim site matched ones a North Korean group used | Security sources: traces linked to several countries, possibly a false flag
col: GMO's position | Its public notices attribute the attack to no one | Kumagai, per Reuters: data reported to authorities; he could not name a country
Kyodo's report cites unnamed sources and IP address overlaps. GMO names no attacker or country.
notes:
There are two accounts of who was behind the attack, and the page keeps them apart.

Kyodo News reported that, according to unnamed sources speaking on November 1, an analysis by GMO found a North Korea-linked group likely responsible, based on traits of the malware and other traces. Kyodo, working with security experts, also found that five IP addresses that reached the claim-of-responsibility site soon after it went online matched addresses previously used by a North Korean group. Cybersecurity sources in the same report said the site carried traces linked to several countries, possibly a false flag.

GMO's public notices attribute the attack to no one. Asked at the November 2 press conference about reports that a North Korea-linked group was likely involved, group CEO Masatoshi Kumagai said GMO had analyzed the affected servers and reported the data to the appropriate authorities, and that with the investigation under way he could not name a country or say anything else, according to Reuters.

When you mention North Korea, keep the word likely, the unnamed sources and the possible false flag with it.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Access | A customer's remote desktop | T1133
step: 02 | Privilege Escalation | An administrator account | T1078
step: 03 | Lateral Movement | The management server | T1072
step: 04 | Credential Access | Logins saved in trading tools | T1555
step: 05 | Collection | Customers' own PCs | T1039
step: 06 | Impact | Trades customers did not place | T1078
step: 07 | Impact | Extortion and a leak site | T1657
Arrows show stage order; GMO has not said how the attacker got in or took the logins.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's seven stages, each with its phase and ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01 is the entry, on August 5, into a customer's remote desktop VPS. Stage 02 is the administrator account and, by August 17, GMO's management server. Stage 03 is that server used as a delivery channel: per @IT, malware went out through it to customer virtual machines from September 20 to October 14. Stage 04 is the broker logins saved in trading tools on those desktops, and stage 05 the files on customers' own PCs, reached through drive redirection. Stage 06 is trades customers did not place, reported from October 21, and stage 07 the extortion demand, the leak site and GMO's own bounty.

The page maps two stages to T1078, Valid Accounts: the administrator account, and the use of the broker logins.

Read the arrows as stage order. GMO has not said how the attacker got in, how the account was obtained, or how the logins were extracted. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== On August 5 the attacker got into a customer's remote desktop VPS
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1133 | External Remote Services
fact: Date | August 5, 2026, in GMO's press conference timeline (@IT)
fact: Entry point | a customer's remote desktop VPS
fact: Method | withheld by GMO
fact: Attacker's claim | a Remote Desktop Protocol flaw, unconfirmed
The T1133 mapping describes the entry point; it is not a confirmed technique.
notes:
Onamae.com Desktop Cloud rented out Windows virtual servers that customers reached over Remote Desktop and used to run FX trading tools such as MetaTrader 4 and 5. A VPS, or virtual private server, is a virtual machine rented to one customer on a provider's hardware, and FX means foreign exchange.

GMO dated the first intrusion to August 5 in the timeline it gave at its November 2 press conference, as reported by @IT, and placed it in a customer's remote desktop VPS. Hirofumi Yamashita, GMO Internet Group's executive vice president in charge of systems, withheld the method, citing the need to prevent similar attacks and the effect on the investigation.

The attacker claimed on its leak site to have exploited a Remote Desktop Protocol flaw, according to GovInfoSecurity, and GMO has not confirmed that. Asked at the press conference whether AI was used in the attack, group CEO Masatoshi Kumagai said there was no evidence that it was.

The page maps this stage to T1133, External Remote Services, and says the mapping describes the entry point and is not a confirmed technique.

== What ATT&CK calls External Remote Services
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1133 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1133/
External Remote Services covers adversaries using external-facing remote services, such as VPNs, to get into a network or stay in it. The page uses it for the entry point; GMO has not said how the attacker got in.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes External Remote Services as adversaries leveraging external-facing remote services to initially access or persist within a network. Remote services such as VPNs, Citrix and other access mechanisms let users connect to internal enterprise network resources from external locations, and remote service gateways often manage the connections and credential authentication for them.

ATT&CK lists the technique under two tactics, Initial Access and Persistence.

On this incident, the page says only that its T1133 mapping describes the entry point, a remote desktop service exposed to customers, and is not a confirmed technique. GMO withheld the method, and the attacker's claim of a Remote Desktop Protocol flaw is unconfirmed.

== An administrator account, then GMO's management server, by August 17
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Privilege Escalation
technique: T1078 | Valid Accounts
fact: Account taken | August 5 or August 17; reports of GMO's timeline differ
fact: Server entered | GMO's management server, August 17, in every report
fact: Attacker's claims | unconfirmed: Active Directory across the VPS network; an intercepted support password
GMO has not said how the account was obtained. That server is the pivot: about five weeks later, the malware went out through it.
notes:
Reports of GMO's press conference timeline differ on when the administrator account was taken. ITmedia Business Online, Business+IT and MediaDogs say the attacker took it at the customer VPS on August 5 and got into GMO's management server on August 17. @IT's account dates the theft of the account to August 17, the same day as the management server intrusion. The page does not choose between them, so give both dates.

GMO has not said how the account was obtained or what kind of account it was. The attacker's version, which GovInfoSecurity reported from the leak site, is that the VPS network ran as an Active Directory environment that let it move to other VPS servers, and that it intercepted an administrator password exposed when a support engineer connected remotely to a customer's PC. GMO has confirmed none of these claims. Active Directory is Microsoft's directory service for identity.

The page maps the stage to T1078, Valid Accounts.

== What ATT&CK calls Valid Accounts
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1078 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078/
Valid Accounts covers adversaries obtaining and abusing the credentials of existing accounts. Compromised credentials may grant increased privilege on specific systems, or access to restricted areas of a network. The page does not say how this account was obtained.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes Valid Accounts as adversaries obtaining and abusing the credentials of existing accounts. Compromised credentials may be used to bypass access controls placed on resources, and may grant an adversary increased privilege to specific systems or access to restricted areas of the network.

ATT&CK lists the technique under four tactics: Stealth, Persistence, Privilege Escalation and Initial Access.

The page maps two stages to it: stage 02, the administrator account, and stage 06, the use of broker logins for trades. For stage 02, GMO has not said how the account was obtained or what kind of account it was.

== Malware went out through the management server, September 20 to October 14
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Lateral Movement
technique: T1072 | Software Deployment Tools
fact: Channel | GMO's management server; mechanism not disclosed
fact: Scale | up to 26,739 VMs (@IT); GMO's notices: servers that may have been affected
fact: Detected | October 20, after a customer inquiry on October 19
The management server is how an intrusion that began in a customer VPS reached other customers' machines.
notes:
The management server is how an intrusion that began in a customer VPS reached other customers' machines. In @IT's account of the press conference, GMO said the malware was distributed through the management server to up to 26,739 customer virtual machines. GMO's written notices use 26,739 as the number of servers that may have been affected, and say it is not the number of cases in which unauthorized access or information acquisition has been confirmed.

GMO has not named the malware or said how the management server delivered it, so the page's T1072 mapping, Software Deployment Tools, rests on GMO's description of the server as the channel.

GMO did not notice for more than two months after August 17. Yamashita said the company's service monitoring had not caught the intrusion and that it would examine what was missing. A customer inquiry on October 19 started the investigation. On October 20 GMO confirmed the malicious program and suspended new sign-ups and the control panel, and from 04:00 JST on October 21 it shut down servers on every plan in sequence.

== What ATT&CK calls Software Deployment Tools
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1072 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1072/
Software Deployment Tools covers adversaries using centralized suites for routine administration, such as configuration management and software deployment tools, to execute commands and move laterally. The page maps stage 03 here because GMO described its management server as the channel.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes Software Deployment Tools as adversaries gaining access to and using centralized software suites installed within an enterprise to execute commands and move laterally through the network. Configuration management and software deployment applications are used for routine administration, in enterprise networks or cloud environments. ATT&CK says access to network-wide or enterprise-wide endpoint management software may enable an adversary to achieve remote code execution on all connected systems.

ATT&CK lists the technique under two tactics, Execution and Lateral Movement. The page places this stage under Lateral Movement.

On this incident, the page says GMO has not named the malware or said how the management server delivered it. The T1072 mapping rests on GMO's description of the server as the channel.

== How an intrusion in a customer's VPS reached other customers' machines
layout: layers
layer: Entry | A customer's remote desktop VPS
layer: Reached | An administrator account | GMO's management server
layer: Malware sent to | Customer virtual machines
layer: Exposed | Saved broker logins | Customers' own PCs, through redirected drives
Arrows show the order of the chain, not how each step was done.
notes:
This slide draws stage 03's point: the management server is how an intrusion that began in a customer VPS reached other customers' machines. Read it from the top, in the order GMO set out at its press conference as reported.

The entry was a customer's remote desktop VPS. From there the attacker held an administrator account and, by August 17, GMO's management server. The two boxes sit side by side because reports differ on when the account was taken. Through that server, malware went out to customer virtual machines.

On those machines were broker logins saved in trading tools, which stage 04 covers. And because Remote Desktop drive sharing was on by default, the drives of the PCs customers connected from were reachable inside their sessions, which stage 05 covers.

The arrows show the order of the layers only. GMO has not said how the account was obtained, how the server delivered the malware, or how the logins were extracted. Keep the 26,739 caveat too: in @IT's account GMO said the malware went to up to 26,739 VMs, and GMO's notices call them servers that may have been affected.

== Broker logins saved in trading tools on the desktops were taken
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1555 | Credentials from Password Stores
fact: Target | logins saved in trading tools such as MT4 and MT5
fact: GMO's wording | may have been obtained and used (second and third reports)
fact: Confirmed | customer information taken outside the company (fourth report)
fact: Not compromised | MT4/MT5 platforms and trading servers, per MetaQuotes
The T1555 mapping assumes saved credentials were read; GMO has not said how.
notes:
GMO's second and third reports said a third party may have obtained trading account login information and other data stored on the servers, including logins saved in trading tools, and used it illegally. Its first report, on October 21, had said that no leak of customers' personal information and no misuse had been confirmed at that point. By the fourth report, on October 24, GMO had confirmed a security problem in the service's system, unauthorized access to its environment, and that customer information was taken outside the company.

MetaQuotes develops MT4 and MT5, the MetaTrader trading platforms. In a notice to brokers that named neither GMO nor any broker, it said neither the platforms nor the trading servers had been compromised, and that attackers had got into rented servers running trading terminals and traded with the logins saved there, according to Finance Magnates.

GMO has not said how the logins were extracted or how data left its network. The page maps the stage to T1555, Credentials from Password Stores, on the assumption that they were read from the trading tools' saved credentials.

== What ATT&CK calls Credentials from Password Stores
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1555 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1555/
Credentials from Password Stores covers adversaries searching common password storage locations. Where passwords sit depends on the operating system or the application holding them. The page's mapping assumes the logins were read from the trading tools' saved credentials.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes Credentials from Password Stores as adversaries searching for common password storage locations to obtain user credentials. Passwords are stored in several places on a system, depending on the operating system or the application holding them, and some applications and services store passwords to make them easier for users to manage, such as password managers and cloud secrets vaults. ATT&CK says credentials obtained this way can be used for lateral movement and to access restricted information.

The technique sits under the Credential Access tactic.

On this incident, GMO has not said how the logins were extracted or how data left its network. The page's T1555 mapping assumes they were read from the trading tools' saved credentials.

== Drive sharing, on by default, exposed files on customers' own PCs
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Collection
technique: T1039 | Data from Network Shared Drive
fact: Feature | RDP drive redirection, on by default (GMO, per @IT)
fact: Files taken | stated by GMO at the press conference (@IT), not in its notices
fact: Device analysis | no malware or ransomware traces so far on the PCs GMO examined
Per @IT, Yamashita said the setting was on for customer convenience, and not switching it off as security risks grew was a lapse in risk analysis.
notes:
When a customer connected, Remote Desktop drive redirection made the drives of that customer's own PC available inside the session on the infected virtual machine. At the press conference GMO named this default as the reason files on customers' local PCs were taken; its written notices do not say so. According to @IT, Yamashita said the setting had been turned on for customer convenience, and that not switching it off as security risks grew was a lapse in risk analysis. Inside the remote session the redirected drives are reachable much like network shares, which is the basis for the page's T1039 mapping.

The attacker had made the drive redirection claim on its leak site before GMO confirmed it, and threatened a ransomware attack with a deadline of noon on October 27. GMO asked customers to back up, disconnect and power off their PCs before then, and collected PCs from customers who volunteered them for analysis. Its fourteenth report, on October 28, said analysis of those devices had found no traces of malware infection or ransomware attacks so far, and its ninth report had cautioned that results to date did not confirm the safety of every customer's device.

== From October 21, customers reported trades they had not placed
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Impact
technique: T1078 | Valid Accounts
fact: First reports | October 21, 2026 (GMO timeline)
fact: FX firms | 5 reported affected customers, per Finance Minister Katayama
fact: GMO asked brokers | to block access through the service and end all sessions
fact: Compensation | realized losses on trades GMO confirms as unauthorized
T1078 describes the logins; the losses themselves are the impact.
notes:
GMO's timeline records reports from October 21 of unauthorized operations on customers' trading accounts. @IT, reporting the press conference, says the attacker used the stolen account information on the same day as the extortion email to place trades that deliberately produced losses in FX accounts. Japan's Finance Minister Satsuki Katayama said five FX firms had reported affected customers, according to Bloomberg as cited by Finance Magnates.

The page maps the step to T1078, Valid Accounts, because MetaQuotes' notice describes trades placed with logins saved on rented servers, and GMO's third report says trading account logins stored on its servers may have been obtained and used illegally.

The extortion message threatened continued unauthorized orders against customer accounts at more than 70 securities and FX companies in Japan and abroad, per GMO's fourth report. GMO asked major exchanges and securities firms to block access coming through the service, forcibly end all active sessions and watch for unauthorized trading. GMO says it will compensate realized losses on trades it confirms were unauthorized, less any refund from the broker, and it began contacting customers about claims on November 6.

== A US$50 million demand, a leak site and GMO's own bounty
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Impact
technique: T1657 | Financial Theft
fact: Demand | US$50 million in Bitcoin or Ether, by 17:00 JST October 21
fact: GMO | puts the demand at about ¥8 billion; it will not pay
fact: Leak | confirmed by GMO at 12:30 JST, October 27
fact: Takedown | site domain suspended around 04:00 JST, October 28
GMO said paying would not guarantee the data was deleted or its misuse stopped.
notes:
The extortion email arrived on October 21, the day GMO suspended the service. The demand, as reproduced on Kumagai's blog, asked for US$50 million in Bitcoin or Ether by 17:00 JST that day; GMO's notices give the amount as about ¥8 billion. The fourth report listed the threats: continued manipulation of trading accounts, publication of information about the service and the customer PCs connected to it along with ransomware attacks, and publication of information about GMO's systems. GMO said it would not pay, because paying would not guarantee that the data was deleted or that its misuse stopped.

At 12:30 JST on October 27 GMO confirmed that the attacker had published what it claimed was information about GMO and its customers, and @IT reports that one record of stolen data appeared. GMO warned that the site captured information about anyone who visited it. The site's domain was suspended at around 04:00 JST on October 28.

The page maps this stage to T1657, Financial Theft. The next slides explain the technique, and separate GMO's bounty from a reported settlement figure.

== What ATT&CK calls Financial Theft
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1657 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1657/
Financial Theft covers adversaries stealing money from targets through extortion, social engineering, technical theft or other methods, for their own gain. ATT&CK calls it the ultimate objective of several campaign types, including business email compromise, fraud and bank hacking.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes Financial Theft as adversaries stealing monetary resources from targets through extortion, social engineering, technical theft or other methods aimed at their own financial gain, at the expense of the availability of those resources for victims. It calls financial theft the ultimate objective of several popular campaign types, among them business email compromise and fraud, and bank hacking.

The technique sits under the Impact tactic.

On this incident, the page maps the extortion stage to T1657. GMO refused to pay the demand.

== Two US$1 million figures: a reported settlement and GMO's bounty
layout: compare
col: A settlement, as reported | GovInfoSecurity: the extortionists later said they would settle for US$1 million | IBTimes, citing NHK: October 27, GMO said it would not pay US$1 million | GMO's own notices give an amount only for the October 21 demand
col: GMO's bounty | Up to US$1 million in total, about ¥150 million | For information leading to identifying and arresting those responsible | Not a ransom or a payment for stolen information, GMO says | 852 tips received by November 6
GMO opened its bounty on October 28, the day the leak site's domain was suspended.
notes:
Two figures of US$1 million appear in the reporting, and they are different things.

The first is a settlement. GovInfoSecurity reported that the extortionists later said they would settle for US$1 million, and IBTimes, citing NHK, reported that on October 27 GMO said it would not pay a US$1 million ransom either. GMO's own notices give an amount only for the October 21 demand.

The second is GMO's own bounty. On October 28, after the leak site's domain was suspended, GMO opened a bounty capped at US$1 million, about ¥150 million, in total, for information leading to the identification and arrest of those responsible. GMO states that the reward is neither a ransom nor a payment for stolen information; it is paid to people who supply information, and the attacker and accomplices are excluded. By November 6 it had received 852 tips.

When you say US$1 million aloud, say which one you mean.

== Six defenses against seven stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06 | 07
row: Drive redirection off by default | 05
row: Change passwords on a clean device, then end sessions | 06
row: Logins that move money off others' desktops | 04
row: Watch the management plane | 02 | 03
row: Penetration test findings: owner above the team, deadline, re-test | -
row: Brokers act on the provider | 06
No defense on the page is tied to stage 01, the entry, or stage 07, the extortion. The penetration test row names no stage.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against the seven stages. A cell is marked only where the defense's instruction names something that stage alone reports; a shared word or a reason is not a tie.

Turning drive redirection off answers stage 05, where redirected drives exposed files on customers' own PCs. Changing broker passwords from a clean device and then ending every session answers stage 06, because some brokers' servers keep sessions alive for a while after a password change and a third party could keep trading. Keeping credentials that move money off desktops other people administer answers stage 04, the broker logins saved in trading tools. Watching the management plane answers stage 02, through an alert on an administrator account used from a customer-facing host, and stage 03, through an alert on software pushed to tenant machines with no matching change. The brokerage defense answers stage 06: block access coming through the service and end active sessions.

The penetration test defense names no stage. GMO has not said whether the issues Ierae found were the ones the attacker used.

Columns 01 and 07 are empty: no defense on the page names the entry or the extortion. That gap is a discussion question at the end.

== Turn drive redirection off; change passwords from a clean device
layout: compare
col: Drive redirection off (stage 05) | GMO said files on customers' PCs were taken because it was on by default | Per Microsoft, the most restrictive setting wins, so block it on the host | Leave drivestoredirect:s: empty in the connection files you hand out | Using someone else's desktop? Set DisableDriveRedirection on your Windows PC
col: Broker passwords (stage 06) | Change the broker password from a device not used for FX trading | Then end every session; some brokers' servers keep them alive for a while | Check trade history; turn on two-step verification where offered | Change passwords stored on the server or saved in a browser too
notes:
Two defenses come straight from stages 05 and 06.

GMO said files on customers' own PCs were taken because Remote Desktop drive sharing was on by default, a setting it had kept for convenience. Microsoft documents that the session host policy "Do not allow drive redirection" wins over a connection setting that allows it, because the most restrictive setting applies, and that an empty drivestoredirect:s: value turns redirection off in the connection files you hand out. If you connect to a desktop someone else runs, Microsoft documents a DisableDriveRedirection registry value that blocks redirection from your own Windows PC.

GMO's guidance set the order for broker passwords: change the password from a device not used for FX trading, then disconnect all active sessions, because some brokers' servers keep existing sessions alive for a while after a password change and a third party could keep trading. The page adds: end the sessions even if the password was already changed, check the trade history, and turn on two-step verification where the broker offers it. Change any password that was stored on the server or saved in a browser too.

== Keep money-moving logins off others' desktops; watch the management plane
layout: compare
col: Money-moving logins (stage 04) | Saved broker logins were within reach of whoever held the management server | Keep logins that move money off desktops other people administer | Where offered, use one-time passwords or passkeys, so saved passwords cannot sign in elsewhere
col: Management plane (stages 02, 03) | GMO said its monitoring missed the intrusion; its notices offer no detection advice | Alert on an administrator account used from a customer-facing host | Alert on software pushed to tenant machines with no matching change
The two alerts are the page's suggestions, drawn from what GMO described.
notes:
Keeping credentials that move money off desktops other people administer answers stage 04. Every broker login saved in a trading tool on these machines was within reach of whoever held GMO's management server. Where a broker offers one-time passwords or passkeys, use them, so that a saved password alone is not enough to sign in from somewhere else. According to Finance Magnates, OANDA Securities plans to make passkeys compulsory, and FXTF said it may switch on one-time passwords for accounts that could have been reached through the service.

Watching the management plane answers stages 02 and 03. GMO's management server was in the attacker's hands from August 17, and malware went out through it for more than three weeks before a customer's question started the investigation. GMO said its monitoring missed this and that it is examining why, and its notices offer no detection advice. The page draws two alerts from what GMO described: an administrator account used from a customer-facing host, and software pushed to tenant machines with no matching change. GMO also decided not to restart the compromised platform and is offering customers a replacement on an independent cloud platform, which avoids trusting a management layer an attacker controlled.

== Give penetration test findings an owner; brokers, act on the provider
layout: compare
col: Owner above the team, deadline, re-test | Group company Ierae flagged issues; diagnosis reported April 24, 2024 | GMO said at the press conference that the fixes lacked depth and speed | Per @IT: remaining work put off, no re-test, findings did not reach Kumagai | GMO has not said whether those issues were the ones the attacker used
col: Brokers (stage 06) | GMO asked major exchanges and brokers to block access and end sessions | FXTF blocked overseas logins; MetaQuotes urged checks on clients using third-party VPS | Block or step up authentication for a breached provider's traffic until customers reset credentials
notes:
Two more defenses: one for whoever commissions penetration tests, and one for brokerages.

GMO Cybersecurity by Ierae, a group company, flagged issues in the service in a diagnosis reported on April 24, 2024. Dempa Shimbun and MediaDogs report four rounds of emergency maintenance between April 26 and May 20, 2024, days after the diagnosis, and ITmedia Business Online, Business+IT and Reuters also place them in April and May 2024; @IT prints the same days with the year 2026. GMO said at the press conference that the fixes lacked depth and speed. Per @IT, the remaining work was put off for the development schedule, the same environment was not re-tested afterwards, and with no escalation criteria the findings did not reach Kumagai. GMO has not said whether the issues Ierae found were the ones the attacker used.

For brokerages, GMO asked major exchanges and securities firms to block access coming through the service and forcibly end active sessions. FXTF blocked logins from overseas IP addresses, and MetaQuotes urged brokers to check clients who trade through third-party VPS services. When a hosting provider your customers trade from is breached, block or step up authentication for traffic from it until those customers have reset their credentials.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Is drive redirection off by default on the hosted desktops you run or use?
check: Would you notice an administrator account used from a customer-facing host?
check: Would you notice software pushed to tenant machines with no matching change?
check: Do any logins that move money sit on a desktop someone else administers?
check: After a password change, do you also end every active session?
check: Do penetration test findings get an owner above the team, a deadline, a re-test?
check: If you run a brokerage, can you step up authentication for one provider's traffic?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

If you run hosted desktops, ask whether drive redirection is off by default, and whether you would notice an administrator account used from a customer-facing host, or software pushed to tenant machines with no matching change. GMO's monitoring did not catch the intrusion for more than two months.

If you use a desktop someone else runs, ask whether any login that moves money is saved on it, whether your broker offers one-time passwords or passkeys, and whether you end every active session after a password change.

Ask who owns each penetration test finding, above the team, with a deadline and a re-test of the same environment.

If you run a brokerage, ask whether you could block or step up authentication for traffic from one hosting provider until its customers have reset their credentials.

== Who else can reach what you keep on a hosted desktop?
layout: question
Per the page, every broker login saved in a trading tool on these desktops was within reach of whoever held GMO's management server. List what you keep on a desktop someone else administers, and what your connection lets it see.
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The question comes from the page's defenses for people who use hosted desktops. Every broker login saved in a trading tool on these machines was within reach of whoever held GMO's management server. With drive redirection on, the drives of the PCs customers connected from were reachable inside the session on the infected virtual machine.

Ask each person to name one thing they keep on a desktop, server or workspace that someone else administers, and one thing their connection to it shares, before you show the answer slide.

== Saved logins, and the drives your session shares
layout: answer
Two exposures: logins saved on the desktop, and, with drive redirection on, files on your PC.
- Keep money-moving logins off others' desktops; use one-time passwords or passkeys where offered.
- Turn drive redirection off on the host and from your PC.
notes:
For people who use a hosted desktop, two of the page's defenses cover two exposures, one on each side of the connection.

On the desktop: every broker login saved in a trading tool was within reach of whoever held GMO's management server. The page's answer is to keep credentials that move money off desktops other people administer, and to use one-time passwords or passkeys where a broker offers them, so that a saved password alone is not enough to sign in from somewhere else.

On the PC you connect from: GMO said files on customers' own PCs were taken because drive redirection was on by default. Microsoft documents a session host policy that blocks it, and a DisableDriveRedirection registry value that blocks redirection from your own Windows PC when you connect to a desktop someone else runs.

Bring the group's examples back to these two.

== What would you add for stages 01 and 07?
layout: question
The page's defenses are tied to stages 02 to 06. None is tied to the entry through a customer's remote desktop, or to the extortion and leak site. What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the gaps in the defense matrix.

The page offers defenses tied to stages 02, 03, 04, 05 and 06, and a penetration test defense that names no stage. It ties nothing to stage 01, the entry into a customer's remote desktop VPS, or to stage 07, the extortion demand, the leak site and the bounty.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught.

== The page ties no defense to stages 01 and 07
layout: answer
The page ties no defense to these stages, and its penetration test advice names no stage: GMO has not said whether the issues Ierae found were the ones the attacker used. Any control you propose is your own suggestion.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. It ties no defense to stage 01 or stage 07, and this deck adds none of its own. The group may argue that the penetration test advice reaches stage 01; the page does not say so, because GMO has not said whether the issues Ierae found were the ones the attacker used.

If the group proposes ideas, treat them as the group's, and test each one against the facts the page reports.

For stage 01, those facts are an entry on August 5 into a customer's remote desktop VPS, a method GMO withheld, and the attacker's unconfirmed claim of a Remote Desktop Protocol flaw.

For stage 07, they are the US$50 million demand of October 21, GMO's refusal to pay, the leak site that GMO warned captured information about anyone who visited it, the suspension of its domain on October 28, and GMO's bounty.

